# About / Summary web–mobile alignment

Channel **About** and item **Summary** must carry the **same information** on web and mobile.
Vocabulary for description / About / Summary lives in **abcmemory-vocabulary**.

## Required content

| Surface | Always includes | Never |
| ------- | --------------- | ----- |
| Channel About (podcast, album, artist) | `channel_description` + `channel_persons` | Falling back to item people |
| Item Summary (episode, track, livestream, full player) | `item_description` + `item_persons` | Falling back to `channel_persons` |

People render whenever the matching person array is non-empty, even if description prose is
empty. Do not hide people behind an empty-description gate.

## Placement divergence (intentional)

Web has more screen width and keeps RSS, website, and funding as **always-visible header
icons**. Mobile tucks RSS and website into **About** so the identity block does not grow an
icon row. Funding is **not** an About body link on mobile: it is its own chip, always the
rightmost chip, and only when that surface has funding rows (channel screens use
`channel_fundings`; item screens use `item_fundings` with no channel fallback).

| Field | Web | Mobile |
| ----- | --- | ------ |
| Description + people | About / Summary body | About / Summary body |
| RSS, website | Header icons | About body links |
| Funding | Header icon | Funding chip (last) |
| Share, subscribe, notifications, boost | Header / actions | Header / actions — not About body |

Do **not** “fix” mobile by copying web header icons for RSS or website, and do **not** drop
those links from mobile About. Do **not** put funding links back into About.

## Adding fields

Adding a new About or Summary field on one surface requires the matching field on the other,
unless the plan records an intentional divergence (same pattern as the RSS/website/funding
placement above).

## Out of scope for this rule

Location, social interact, license, trailer, and other podcast-namespace tags are stored but
not part of the About/Summary body contract until both surfaces adopt them together.
